## Domain 1: Agents and Workflows (Q1.1–Q1.9)

### Q1.1 · Agent Architecture
A team builds an assistant that takes a support ticket and must: pull the customer's  
account, check entitlement, and draft one of three pre-approved responses depending on  
entitlement tier. The three response types are exhaustive and the branching logic is simple  
and fully known in advance. An engineer proposes a single autonomous agent with tool access  
that plans its own path through the task on each ticket.

A) Correct as proposed — autonomous planning is the safer default whenever multiple tools  
   are involved, regardless of how well the branching is understood in advance.
B) A fixed workflow (chained calls with a deterministic branch on entitlement tier) fits  
   better — the paths are fully enumerable and known, so agentic planning overhead buys  
   nothing here.
C) A manager/supervisor hierarchy, delegating "check entitlement" and "draft response" to  
   separate subagents, since decomposition always improves reliability for multi-step tasks.
D) A single subagent invoked via `Task` for the whole ticket, since subagent isolation  
   prevents the entitlement check from being contaminated by drafting logic.

### Q1.2 · Agent Architecture
A supervisor agent routes requests across three specialist subagents (billing, shipping,  
returns) and combines their outputs into one reply. On a request that clearly needs only the  
shipping specialist, the supervisor still invokes all three "to be safe" before assembling a  
response, tripling latency and cost for single-domain requests.

A) Remove the supervisor and let a single generalist agent handle all requests directly,  
   since supervisors add overhead this scenario shows isn't worth it.
B) Have the supervisor classify the request's domain(s) first and route only to the  
   subagent(s) that classification indicates, invoking multiple specialists only when a  
   request genuinely spans domains.
C) Keep invoking all three every time, but run them in parallel instead of sequentially,  
   since parallel execution removes the latency cost entirely.
D) Merge the three specialists into one subagent with a larger combined toolset, since a  
   single broader specialist is always more efficient than several narrow ones.

### Q1.3 · Agent Architecture
A single agent handles both "look up my order status" (one tool call, done) and "figure out  
why 40% of orders from Region X are arriving late" (open-ended, requires multiple rounds of  
exploration across several data sources, with the next query depending on what the last one  
found). The team wants to know when delegating the second kind of request to a subagent via  
`Task` is actually justified.

A) Never — subagent delegation only adds context-loading overhead and should be avoided for  
   any single-session assistant.
B) Always — every tool call should route through a subagent regardless of complexity, for  
   consistency of architecture.
C) When the sub-task needs independent, expendable context (heavy exploration that would  
   otherwise crowd the main thread) or parallelizable, hand-off-and-return work — the  
   open-ended investigation qualifies; the order lookup doesn't.
D) Only when the sub-task touches a different tool server than the main agent's toolset,  
   since subagents exist to solve cross-server authentication issues.

### Q1.4 · Agent Construction with Claude
A team builds a coding agent on the Claude Agent SDK for an unattended overnight refactor  
job. They're worried about a run that gets stuck reasoning in circles on an ambiguous merge  
conflict and silently consumes hours of compute before anyone notices.

A) Poll a separate monitoring dashboard every few minutes and manually kill the process if  
   token spend looks unusually high.
B) Set `max_turns` and `max_budget_usd` in the agent options, and check the `ResultMessage`  
   for `error_max_turns` / `error_max_budget_usd` when the run ends, so the loop is bounded  
   and the reason is programmatically visible.
C) Ask the model in the system prompt to stop and summarize if it notices it's been  
   reasoning for a long time without progress.
D) Lower `max_tokens` per turn so each individual response is short, which caps the total  
   compute the loop can consume.

### Q1.5 · Agent Construction with Claude
A finance-ops agent can call a tool that issues wire transfers. The team wants a guarantee  
that no transfer above $10,000 executes without a human clicking "approve" first — including  
during unattended batch runs where the model might otherwise "helpfully" proceed on its own  
judgment that a transfer looks routine.

A) Add a system-prompt instruction: "Always pause and ask for confirmation before any  
   transfer over $10,000."
B) Implement a hook that intercepts the transfer tool call, checks the amount, and blocks or  
   holds the call pending an external approval signal when the threshold is exceeded —  
   enforcement outside the model's own judgment.
C) Lower the model's temperature so it behaves more conservatively around large transfers.
D) Remove the wire-transfer tool from the agent's toolset entirely, and have a human execute  
   every transfer manually regardless of amount.

### Q1.6 · Agent Construction with Claude
A five-person startup has a working agent prototype validated in dev. They have no SRE  
function, no on-call rotation, and no plans to hire one soon, but they do need the agent  
live for real customers within two weeks and want to retain the ability to swap models or  
adjust runtime behavior later without renegotiating a platform contract.

A) Self-host on their own Kubernetes cluster from day one, since owning the full stack from  
   the start avoids costly migrations later.
B) Use an Anthropic-hosted managed deployment now to get to production fast without building  
   ops capacity, accepting less low-level runtime control in exchange for speed — and  
   revisit self-hosting later if their needs outgrow it.
C) Wait to launch until they've hired a platform engineer, since production AI agents cannot  
   responsibly run without dedicated infrastructure staff.
D) Self-host, but outsource on-call to a third-party MSP immediately, since a managed  
   deployment cannot be swapped or adjusted later.

### Q1.7 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks
An engineering team evaluating LangGraph for a new multi-agent project argues: "Once we  
adopt an established agentic framework, we can skip building our own evals — the framework's  
own test suite already proves the pattern works, so our specific implementation inherits  
that guarantee."

A) Correct — frameworks like LangGraph, Strands, and PydanticAI are pre-validated, so evals  
   on the application built with them are redundant.
B) Incorrect — frameworks provide reusable scaffolding for the tool-use loop, state  
   handling, and multi-step/multi-agent orchestration patterns, but they don't validate the  
   team's specific prompts, tools, or task logic; evals are still required for the actual  
   application.
C) Correct, but only for single-agent graphs — multi-agent graphs still require the team's  
   own evals.
D) Incorrect, because these frameworks explicitly disable evaluation tooling by design to  
   prevent redundant testing.

### Q1.8 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks
A meal-planning assistant needs to recall, across sessions weeks apart, that a specific user  
is vegetarian and dislikes cilantro. The team's current implementation relies on the model  
"remembering" this from earlier in a very long-lived single conversation that's never  
actually closed.

A) Keep the single long-lived conversation open indefinitely, since closing and reopening  
   sessions is what causes memory loss in the first place.
B) Increase the context window so the standing preferences are less likely to be pushed out  
   of the effective window as the conversation grows.
C) Persist the preferences to an external memory store, and load only the relevant facts  
   into context at the start of each new session, rather than relying on an ever-growing  
   single conversation to carry them.
D) Re-ask the user for their preferences every session, since re-collection is more reliable  
   than any storage mechanism.

### Q1.9 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks *(select TWO)*
A research agent accumulates full raw tool outputs (large JSON payloads, full page text from  
web fetches) over a 90-minute session. By minute 60, the model starts producing  
lower-quality answers and occasionally loses track of an early, important finding.

Select the **two** patterns that directly address this:

A) Prune and compact: strip or summarize spent tool outputs after they've served their  
   purpose, keeping decisions and key facts while dropping bulk raw payloads.
B) Persist load-bearing facts to a separately-maintained scratchpad/case-facts record that's  
   re-read rather than relying on it staying discoverable in raw history.
C) Switch to a model with a larger context window so all the raw payloads fit without  
   needing to prune anything.
D) Lower the temperature so the model reasons more carefully over the accumulated context.
E) Add more few-shot examples demonstrating how to synthesize findings from long contexts.

