cost/quality dashboards, triaging user-reported miscategorizations, shipping small prompt  
fixes, and now planning a deliberate migration to a newer model snapshot after validating it  
against their eval suite.

A) Requirements analysis — they're gathering new requirements from user feedback.
B) System design — model migration is a design-phase activity.
C) Operations and maintenance — sustaining a live system's health, correcting defects, and  
   performing controlled upgrades is the defining work of this life-cycle phase.
D) Implementation — the prompt fixes count as new implementation work, placing this squarely  
   in the build phase.

### Q2.4 · Systems Life Cycle
A team is deciding between a **waterfall-style** rollout (fully spec the assistant's  
behavior, build it end-to-end, then release once) and an **iterative** rollout (release a  
narrow-scope version, gather real usage data, and expand scope in short cycles) for a novel  
Claude-powered feature where nobody on the team has previously built with LLMs and the  
correct prompt/tool design isn't yet known.

A) Waterfall is preferable here, since a complete upfront specification avoids the cost of  
   mid-project rework entirely.
B) Iterative is preferable here: LLM application behavior is discovered empirically through  
   real usage far more than it can be fully specified upfront, so short feedback cycles  
   reduce the risk of building the wrong thing at scale.
C) Neither matters — life-cycle methodology has no bearing on LLM-based systems  
   specifically.
D) Waterfall is preferable specifically because Claude's API is stable enough that  
   requirements won't change once specified.

### Q2.5 · Claude API Mechanics
An application needs sentiment labels for 2 million archived tickets as a one-time backfill;  
results are fine within 24 hours, and per-request cost at that volume is the dominant  
business concern. The team is currently running this through the synchronous Messages API  
one ticket at a time and hitting cost and throughput limits.

A) Parallelize the synchronous calls across many more concurrent workers, since concurrency  
   resolves both the cost and throughput concerns simultaneously.
B) Switch to the Message Batches API — purpose-built for high-volume, latency-insensitive  
   workloads, offering a completion window (currently within 24 hours) at meaningfully lower  
   per-token cost than the synchronous path.
C) Keep the synchronous path but enable streaming, since streaming reduces the effective  
   cost per request.
D) Reduce the model's `max_tokens` per request, since output length is the primary driver of  
   the observed cost at this volume.

### Q2.6 · Claude API Mechanics
A developer wants a chat application to render Claude's reply progressively as it's  
generated, rather than waiting for the full response, because perceived latency (not total  
generation time) is the complaint from users. Separately, a different workload needs to  
process a large one-time backfill where nobody is watching a screen in real time.

A) Enable streaming for the chat application; use the Batches API for the backfill — each  
   fits the workload's actual latency sensitivity.
B) Enable streaming for both workloads, since streaming universally reduces cost regardless  
   of whether anyone is watching in real time.
C) Use the Batches API for both workloads, since batch processing is strictly more efficient  
   whenever more than one request is involved.
D) Streaming and batch processing are mutually exclusive alternatives to the same problem,  
   so either one alone resolves both workloads equally well.

