### Q2.9 · Software Engineering Foundations
A service calls the Messages API from a Python backend and needs to process 300 independent  
documents per run without blocking the event loop for other work the service also needs to  
do concurrently (serving other requests, running health checks).

A) Use a synchronous `for` loop calling the API once per document, since synchronous code is  
   simpler to reason about and reliability matters more than throughput here.
B) Use async I/O with a bounded concurrency limit (e.g., a semaphore) so many requests are  
   in flight at once without exceeding account rate limits, and without blocking the rest of  
   the service's event loop.
C) Spawn one operating-system thread per document regardless of count, since threads always  
   outperform async I/O for network-bound work in Python.
D) Move the entire service, including its other concurrent responsibilities, onto the  
   Message Batches API, since batch processing eliminates the need for async programming  
   anywhere in the service.

