   developer's personal `CLAUDE.md`.

### Q2.15 · Claude Application Design
A tool's JSON schema defines `refund_amount` as a required `number` field. A separate  
concern in the same application: the same underlying "refund" capability is invoked  
identically whether a human clicks a UI button (which then calls the API directly) or the  
agent decides autonomously to issue a refund mid-conversation — with no difference in how  
carefully the amount is validated in either path.

A) This is fine as designed, since the tool call is identical either way and downstream  
   validation doesn't need to distinguish who initiated it.
B) The two paths should be designed with different risk postures in mind: a human-initiated,  
   UI-driven call to a backend is a different trust boundary than a model autonomously  
   deciding to invoke the same action, and the schema/approval flow should reflect that  
   (e.g., extra confirmation gating for the agent-initiated path).
C) The schema is the sole point of control here, so making `refund_amount` an `integer`  
   instead of `number` resolves the concern.
D) Content boundaries (delimiting untrusted text) are the relevant fix, since this is  
   fundamentally a prompt-injection scenario.

### Q2.16 · Configuration Management
A repo's `CLAUDE.md` states coding conventions. A separate `settings.json` in the same repo  
defines `allowed-tools` and hook registrations. A new engineer proposes moving the  
`allowed-tools` list into `CLAUDE.md` "to keep all configuration in one readable file."

A) Reasonable — `CLAUDE.md` and `settings.json` are interchangeable containers, so  
   consolidating is purely a readability improvement with no functional difference.
B) Not appropriate — `CLAUDE.md` is natural-language project context and conventions the  
   model reads, while `settings.json` holds structured, enforced configuration (permissions,  
   hooks) the harness reads; moving `allowed-tools` into `CLAUDE.md` would make it advisory  
   text rather than an enforced constraint.
C) Reasonable, but only because `allowed-tools` is deprecated in favor of `CLAUDE.md`-based  
   tool policies in current Claude Code versions.
D) Not appropriate, because `CLAUDE.md` cannot be checked into version control the way  
   `settings.json` can.

