## Answer Key & Rationale — Set 1

### Domain 1

**Q1.1 — B.** Fully enumerable, known-in-advance branching is the textbook case for a fixed  
workflow; agentic planning overhead buys nothing when the paths never actually vary. *(A/D  
overapply autonomy/isolation to a task that needs neither; C adds coordination overhead with  
no distinct specializations to justify it.)*

**Q1.2 — B.** Route by classification first; invoke multiple specialists only when a request  
genuinely spans domains — that's the supervisor doing its actual job. *(A discards a working  
pattern instead of fixing its usage; C treats parallelism as free, ignoring the unnecessary  
cost/noise of invoking irrelevant specialists; D assumes a merged generalist is always  
better, which erases the benefit of narrow specialization.)*

**Q1.3 — C.** Subagent delegation earns its overhead for genuinely independent,  
expendable-context, or parallelizable work — the open-ended investigation qualifies, the  
single lookup doesn't. *(A and B are absolutes that ignore the real distinction; D invents  
an unrelated criterion — cross-server auth isn't what subagents solve.)*

**Q1.4 — B.** `max_turns`/`max_budget_usd` plus checking the `ResultMessage`'s error subtype  
is the SDK-native, deterministic way to bound and detect an overrun. *(A is manual and  
reactive; C is a soft, unenforceable self-check; D caps individual response length, not  
total loop iterations.)*

**Q1.5 — B.** A hook enforces the threshold outside the model's own judgment — the only way  
to guarantee it holds even when the model "helpfully" thinks a transfer looks routine. *(A  
and C are advisory/probabilistic; D removes a needed capability instead of gating it  
properly.)*

**Q1.6 — B.** Managed hosting trades some runtime control for speed — exactly the tradeoff a  
small team without ops capacity should take, with the option to reconsider later. *(A and D  
commit to operational overhead the team explicitly can't support yet; C is an unnecessary  
blocker — managed offerings exist for exactly this situation.)*

**Q1.7 — B.** Frameworks supply orchestration scaffolding, not validation of a specific  
application's prompts/tools/logic — evals remain necessary regardless. *(A, C, and D all  
treat framework adoption as a substitute for evaluation, which it isn't, at any scope.)*

**Q1.8 — C.** External persistent storage, loaded selectively at session start, is the  
standard cross-session memory pattern. *(A and B keep relying on an ever-growing single  
conversation, which doesn't scale and isn't true persistence; D discards a solvable problem  
onto the user.)*

**Q1.9 — A and B.** Compaction/pruning removes stale bulk from context; a separate  
case-facts scratchpad protects specific load-bearing findings from being lost regardless of  
what else gets pruned or summarized — two independent, complementary fixes. *(C trades a  
real fix for more headroom that will eventually fill again; D and E don't address the actual  
mechanism — bloated, stale context, not insufficient care or insufficient examples.)*

### Domain 2

**Q2.1 — B.** The first is a functional requirement (a described behavior); the second  
constrains capacity/latency, which is an infrastructure requirement. *(A and C collapse a  
real distinction; D discards a legitimate functional requirement.)*

**Q2.2 — B and E.** Both describe performance/capacity/availability constraints on the  
system rather than specific behaviors or business goals. *(A and D are functional  
requirements — specific behaviors; C is a business goal the requirements exist to serve, not  
a requirement itself.)*

**Q2.3 — C.** Monitoring, defect triage, prompt fixes, and a validated, controlled model  
migration are the defining activities of operations and maintenance. *(A, B, and D describe  
pre-launch or build-phase activities that are complete by this point.)*

**Q2.4 — B.** When correct prompt/tool behavior isn't yet known, short iterative cycles  
surface that knowledge from real usage far faster and more safely than a complete upfront  
spec. *(A and D assume upfront certainty the team explicitly doesn't have; C wrongly claims  
life-cycle methodology is irrelevant to LLM systems.)*

**Q2.5 — B.** High volume, latency-tolerant, cost-dominant — exactly the Batches API's  
designed use case. *(A pays full synchronous cost for speed nobody needs; C and D  
misattribute the cost driver to streaming or output length rather than the volume/pricing  
model itself.)*

**Q2.6 — A.** Each workload gets the delivery model that matches its actual latency  
sensitivity — streaming for perceived responsiveness, batch for unattended volume. *(B, C,  
and D each force one mechanism onto a workload it doesn't fit, or falsely claim the two are  
interchangeable.)*

**Q2.7 — B.** Claude is available through major cloud platforms, letting a team keep its  
existing procurement/governance/audit tooling while still using Claude. *(A and C are  
factually wrong; D assumes an automatic outcome that still requires actual configuration.)*

**Q2.8 — B.** Images go in as image content blocks, documents as text, and extended thinking  
is an independent lever for reasoning depth, not a format requirement. *(A and C  
misunderstand native multi-format input support; D invents a restriction that doesn't  
exist.)*

**Q2.9 — B.** Bounded-concurrency async I/O gets throughput without blocking the rest of the  
service or busting rate limits. *(A leaves throughput on the table for no reliability  
benefit given independent documents; C ignores that async, not raw threading, is the  
